<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead"><strong>Convert every quote to a price per hour before you compare it, then check the off-peak rate before you negotiate anything.</strong> Across the 18 UK venues we surveyed that sell a comparable court, the median was £13.45 an hour and half sat between £12.00 and £15.00. But one centre advertising £11.60 is the fourth cheapest on paper and the fourth dearest in reality, because it sells 45 minutes. And where off-peak prices are published they run 14 to 34 per cent below peak, which beats every club discount in our survey and requires no conversation at all.</p>

      <p>Get this wrong and you find out in August: the renewal lands, it is up again, and the committee starts talking about putting subs up rather than asking why nobody read the council's fees paper in February.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:30px">
      <h4>The short version</h4>
      <ul style="margin:10px 0 0">
        <li><strong>The headline rate is the least comparable number you have.</strong> Slot lengths run from 45 to 60 minutes, and some centres charge per player rather than per court.</li>
        <li><strong>Check off-peak first.</strong> Where it is published it runs 14 to 34 per cent below peak. It is the largest discount in our survey and the only one you do not have to ask for.</li>
        <li><strong>Ten sessions is the number that matters twice.</strong> It is the threshold for HMRC's block-booking VAT exemption and, at some centres, for a commercial discount.</li>
        <li><strong>Ask whether the hall is a sports facility or a bare hall.</strong> A village, church or school hall let bare is VAT-exempt already. Asking them to put the posts and nets out can change that.</li>
        <li><strong>Whole-hall hire only ever beats taking every court.</strong> At two venues the hall costs less than all five of its courts and at two more it costs exactly the same. Take one court fewer and the hall is dearer everywhere we looked.</li>
        <li><strong>The club rate is real and mostly unpublished.</strong> Where centres publish one it runs 16 to 25 per cent below casual. Most say "contact us", which is the point.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:22px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem">Written for UK club organisers hiring courts from a leisure centre, school or university. Figures come from a survey of 24 venues whose published prices we read on 26 August 2026; 18 sell a comparable per-court unit. Seventeen of those are on 2026/27 prices and one on 2025/26, which is flagged where it matters. The VAT section follows HMRC's published guidance and is not tax advice.</p>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:26px">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#unit">What are you actually buying?</a>
      <a href="#what-to-pay">What should you expect to pay?</a>
      <a href="#drivers">What drives the difference?</a>
      <a href="#off-peak">Is off-peak cheaper, and can your club use it?</a>
      <a href="#block">Does block booking get you a discount?</a>
      <a href="#vat">Is VAT charged on court hire?</a>
      <a href="#negotiable">What else is negotiable besides the rate?</a>
      <a href="#whole-hall">Is the whole hall cheaper than the courts inside it?</a>
      <a href="#who">Who do you talk to, and when?</a>
      <a href="#cua">Is the venue bound by a community use agreement?</a>
      <a href="#terms">Which contract terms matter as much as the rate?</a>
      <a href="#ready">What do you need ready before you ask?</a>
      <a href="#walk-away">When should you walk away?</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <h2 id="unit">What are you actually buying?</h2>
      <p>You are buying a court for a slot that is not always an hour, sometimes priced per player rather than per court, and not always including everyone who walks on with you.</p>
      <p><strong>Slot length is not standard.</strong> Castle Leisure Centre in Bury sells a 45-minute court and Halton Borough Council sells 55 minutes. Every other comparable venue in our survey sells a full hour. Nobody labels the short slot as anything, and it moves the real price by up to a third.</p>
      <p><strong>Some centres charge per player.</strong> The PEAK in Stirling prices badminton at £6.50 per adult with a minimum of two players, which for a club running doubles is roughly £26 a court-hour against a £13.45 median. A club that budgets per court and books a per-player venue has doubled its cost before anyone picks up a racket.</p>
      <p><strong>And "a court" does not always mean four people on it.</strong> Better's Brook Leisure Centre in Belfast states that the initial booking covers one player and reserves the court, with additional players paying separately at reception. Bury charges a guest fee of half the court fee for non-member additional players.</p>

      <h2 id="what-to-pay">What should you expect to pay?</h2>
      <p>Expect a median of £13.45 per court per hour, with half the venues we surveyed sitting between £12.00 and £15.00.</p>
      <p>No published national figure for badminton court hire exists, so we made one. On 26 August 2026 we read the published rate cards and booking pages of 24 UK venues across councils, leisure trusts, universities, schools and one independent school's sports centre. Eighteen sell a comparable per-court unit; the other six sell something else, which is a finding in its own right.</p>

