      <p style="margin-top:22px"><strong>The two rows in bold are the point of the whole table.</strong> Bury advertises £11.60, the fourth cheapest headline price in the survey. Converted to an hour it is £15.47, the fourth most expensive. Halton advertises £14.50 and moves from thirteenth to sixteenth the same way, which makes it the third dearest court in the survey.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/court-hire-slot-length.svg" alt="Two ranked columns of the same eighteen venues. On the left they are ordered by advertised price, and Castle Leisure Centre in Bury sits fourth from cheapest at eleven pounds sixty. On the right they are ordered by real price per hour, and Bury has moved to fourth from most expensive at fifteen pounds forty-seven, because it sells a forty-five minute court. Halton makes a similar move for the same reason, from thirteenth to sixteenth, selling fifty-five minutes at fifteen pounds eighty-two." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">The same eighteen venues, ranked by what they advertise and by what they actually charge per hour. Both venues selling under an hour finish in the four most expensive.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p style="margin-top:22px">Divide the price by the minutes and multiply by sixty. That is the whole technique, and it is the only way two quotes mean the same thing.</p>
      <p>Two caveats we will not bury. One of the eighteen rows is an upper bound rather than a price, because Botwell Green publishes "up to £12.70"; leaving it out moves the median to £13.65 and the upper quartile to £15.13, and leaves the lower quartile untouched. And one venue is still on 2025/26 prices where the rest are on 2026/27.</p>

      <h2 id="drivers">What drives the difference?</h2>
      <p>Operator type is the signal you can see, and region is not separable from it at any sample a club could reasonably gather.</p>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-top:16px">
        <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.95rem">
          <thead>
            <tr style="text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--line)">
              <th style="padding:8px 10px">Operator</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">Venues</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">Median</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">Range</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">University</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">3</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£10.00</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£9.00 to £15.00</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">School</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">1</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£10.50</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">-</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Trust or charity</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">4</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£13.35</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£12.00 to £15.00</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Council</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">9</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£13.65</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£12.00 to £16.00</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:8px 10px">Independent school centre</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">1</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£16.20</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">-</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:18px">The three cheapest venues in the survey are two universities and a school hall. That is a real signal and it is worth acting on. It is not the same as saying education is cheaper, though, and we should be precise about the difference: Brunel University charges £15.00, above six of the nine councils, and the most expensive venue in the whole survey is an independent school's own sports centre.</p>
      <p>We are not going to tell you how much of the variation is region, because our data cannot support it. All three North West venues are council-run, the only Welsh venue is a university, and the sample is eighteen. Any percentage we attached would be invented. Look at who runs the building before you look at where it is, and then go and check the specific places near you.</p>

      <h2 id="off-peak">Is off-peak cheaper, and can your club use it?</h2>
      <p>Off-peak is the largest published discount in this survey, running 14 to 34 per cent below peak at the four venues that publish both.</p>
      <p>Bury drops from £15.47 to £13.33 an hour, Sefton from £16.00 to £13.50, Benham from £12.00 to £10.00, and Halton from £15.82 to £10.47. That last one is a third off, and it is larger than Halton's own club rate, which is the point: the discount you have to ask for is smaller than the one already printed on the price list.</p>
      <p><strong>Nobody negotiates for this and everybody could.</strong> It is on the price list, it requires no conversation, and most clubs never look at it because the club night has always been on a Tuesday at eight.</p>
      <p>The catch is real and it is about your members, not your budget. Off-peak usually means daytime or late evening, which suits retired players, shift workers, students and parents during school hours, and suits nobody in full-time work who wants to play at eight. For most clubs this is not a saving they can take. For a club with a daytime section, a juniors slot or an over-60s group, it is the cheapest court time available and it is sitting in plain sight.</p>

      <h2 id="block">Does block booking get you a discount?</h2>
      <p>Where a centre publishes a club or block rate the discount is large, and most centres do not publish one at all.</p>
      <p>Halton Borough Council publishes badminton club hire at £10.90 for a 55-minute court against £14.50 casual, which converts to £11.89 against £15.82 an hour: a quarter off, for being a club.</p>
      <p><strong>Watch how block rates are listed.</strong> Tendring District Council's published block rate is £14.40, higher than its casual rate of £13.65. The 20 per cent discount for ten or more sessions on one invoice is applied separately, bringing it to £11.52, about 16 per cent below casual. A club comparing headline numbers would conclude that block booking costs more.</p>
      <p>Everywhere else the club rate exists in some form and is not published. North Yorkshire refers to "reduced rates for block and club bookings" without a figure. Codsall and Wombourne both direct you to email. Better's Belfast centres take block bookings by enquiry only. We cannot tell you whether each of those centres holds a standard club rate or simply prices case by case, and neither can anyone else from the outside, which is the useful part: an unpublished price is one that gets set in a conversation.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>When to stop negotiating</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">A guide like this can leave you feeling you should always be pushing. You should not.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:8px"><strong>If your normalised rate is at or below £12.00 an hour, you are in the cheapest quarter of everything we surveyed.</strong> If you also already hold a club or block rate, there is realistically nothing left on the table, and the time is better spent on filling the courts you have than on shaving the price of them.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="vat">Is VAT charged on court hire?</h2>
      <p>It depends on what kind of room you are hiring, not just on how you book it, and that is the part almost every guide skips.</p>
      <p><strong>Start with the building.</strong> HMRC treats letting facilities for playing sport as normally standard-rated at 20 per cent. But a general purpose hall, such as a village or church hall, that merely has floor markings is not classed as a sports facility, and letting it is exempt even when it is let for playing sport. School halls are treated the same way, provided it is the bare hall being supplied. Gymnasiums are excluded from that treatment.</p>
      <p><strong>And here is the trap, which sits directly on advice this guide gives elsewhere.</strong> If equipment such as racquets and nets is provided along with the hall, the supply becomes standard-rated sports facilities. So asking the village hall to put the posts and nets out for you can convert an exempt let into a taxable one. If you hire a bare hall and bring your own posts, ask before you change that arrangement.</p>
      <p><strong>Now the booking.</strong> For a genuine sports facility, a series of ten or more sessions can be exempt, unless the provider has opted to tax. All of the following must be true: ten or more sessions; the same sport each time; the same place; an interval of at least one day and no more than 14 between sessions; the series paid for as a whole with written evidence; the hirer a school, club or association; and exclusive use during each session.</p>
      <p>Three of those conditions bite in practice. HMRC states plainly that there is no exception for an interval over 14 days caused by the facility closing for any reason, so a school-holiday break can end your series. The payment condition means the evidence must show payment is due whether or not each session is used, although a refund for the provider's own unforeseen non-availability does not affect this. And "unless the provider has opted to tax" is not a footnote: a commercial landlord who has opted to tax charges VAT regardless of your series.</p>
      <p><strong>What all this does not mean is that qualifying takes 20 per cent off your invoice.</strong> Whether exemption changes your price depends on who you are hiring from:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A commercial operator</strong> currently charging you standard-rated hire: a qualifying series should remove the VAT, unless they have opted to tax. Whether the saving reaches you or is retained in their margin is a commercial question, not a tax one.</li>
        <li><strong>A local authority:</strong> possibly nothing changes. Since Revenue and Customs Brief 3 (2023), HMRC has accepted that councils' leisure services can be treated as non-business and outside the scope of VAT, but that treatment is conditional and is adopted authority by authority rather than automatically. Contracted-out leisure services are a different case again.</li>
        <li><strong>A charitable leisure trust:</strong> there may be a second route open to you. The sports exemption for eligible bodies applies to supplies made to individuals taking part, and HMRC's definition of "individual" expressly includes an unincorporated association booking on behalf of members, where the supplier is non-profit-making and the true beneficiaries are the people playing. That describes a lot of clubs hiring from a lot of trusts. It is worth asking about alongside the series-of-lets route rather than instead of it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>So do not walk in asking for 20 per cent off. Ask three questions instead: <em>is our booking standard-rated at the moment</em>, <em>are we hiring a sports facility or a bare hall</em>, and <em>if we structure it as a series of ten or more sessions on one invoice, does that change the treatment?</em> Those are answerable by the person who raises your invoice.</p>
      <p>That the series rule shows up in real pricing is not theoretical. Sefton's published fees schedule notes that exemption applies to individuals and not-for-profit groups, "or when the block booking exemption can be applied", with standard rating otherwise.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>Worked example: a club at Wombourne, 4 courts, 2 hours, 38 weeks</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Wombourne Leisure Centre publishes £12.50 a court and £59.00 for the whole five-court hall.</p>
        <ul style="margin:12px 0 0">
          <li>Four courts for two hours: <strong>4 &times; 2 &times; £12.50 = £100.00 a week</strong>, or £3,800 over a 38-week season.</li>
          <li>All five courts for two hours: <strong>5 &times; 2 &times; £12.50 = £125.00</strong>, so the hall at <strong>£59.00 &times; 2 = £118.00</strong> saves £7.00 a week if you wanted all five anyway.</li>
          <li>But against the four courts you actually need, the hall costs <strong>£18.00 a week more</strong>. The fifth court and exclusive use are worth £18.00 a week to you, or they are not.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:12px">And the VAT? Wombourne is a district council leisure centre, so the supply may already be outside the scope under the 2023 position, in which case there is no 20 per cent to remove whatever your booking looks like. Ask them; do not assume it either way.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="negotiable">What else is negotiable besides the rate?</h2>
      <p>More is negotiable than the rate, though we should be straight about the evidence: no operator we could find publishes its block-booking terms, so this is a list of things worth asking for rather than a description of what is normal.</p>
      <p>Worth asking for:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A fixed court allocation and slot each week</strong>, rather than a floating one.</li>
        <li><strong>Storage</strong> for posts, nets and a first-aid kit, which removes a job somebody currently does every week.</li>
        <li><strong>Access and setup time</strong>, and whether your two hours includes putting the posts out. Check the VAT point above before asking them to supply equipment.</li>
        <li><strong>A one-off tournament</strong> on your usual night without being re-charged as a new booking.</li>
        <li><strong>What happens to your slot in the school holidays</strong>, which is also a VAT question if you are relying on a series.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>And the lever most clubs never use: offer something back.</strong> A centre or school is not only selling court time, it has its own objectives, and a club can meet them cheaply. A taster session for the school's pupils, an after-school club, a junior section that feeds the venue's own programmes, or simply filling a slot that is otherwise dead are all worth real money to the person you are negotiating with. This is the only lever available to a club with no alternative venue, and it is the one this guide would push hardest.</p>

      <h2 id="whole-hall">Is the whole hall cheaper than the courts inside it?</h2>
      <p>The whole hall can be cheaper than taking every court in it, but it is dearer than taking one court fewer at every venue we surveyed, so the arithmetic decides it.</p>
      <p>Multiply the per-court rate by the number of courts in the hall and compare it to the whole-hall price:</p>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-top:16px">
        <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.95rem">
          <thead>
            <tr style="text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--line)">
              <th style="padding:8px 10px">Venue</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">Whole hall</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">Every court</th><th style="padding:8px 10px">One court fewer</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Codsall (5 courts)</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£59.00</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£62.50 - <strong>hall cheaper</strong></td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£50.00 - hall dearer</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Wombourne (5 courts)</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£59.00</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£62.50 - <strong>hall cheaper</strong></td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£50.00 - hall dearer</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)"><td style="padding:8px 10px">Newcastle University (8)</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£80.00</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£80.00 - level</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£70.00 - hall dearer</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding:8px 10px">Plympton Academy (4)</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£42.00</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£42.00 - level</td><td style="padding:8px 10px">£31.50 - hall dearer</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:18px">Three other venues in our survey publish a whole-hall price and no court count, so their halls cannot be converted at all and we have not guessed at them.</p>
      <p>What the table says is narrower than the usual advice. If you want every court in the hall, ask for the hall price, because at two of four venues it is cheaper and at the other two it costs the same while adding exclusive use. If you want fewer courts than the hall holds, the hall is dearer, and the question becomes what exclusivity is worth to you: no five-a-side in the next bay, no pay-and-play queue at the door, and room to spread out when forty people turn up instead of thirty.</p>

      <h2 id="who">Who do you talk to, and when?</h2>
      <p>Talk to a named administrator or centre manager rather than the booking desk, and do it months before April when new prices take effect.</p>
      <p>Centres route block enquiries away from reception. LeisurePlex in Lisburn directs block bookings to a named administrator, and Codsall and Wombourne both direct them to the centre's email rather than its phone line. The inference we draw from that, and it is an inference rather than something anyone publishes, is that the booking desk holds a price list rather than any authority to depart from it.</p>
      <p><strong>The timing matters more than the routing, at councils specifically.</strong> Council leisure prices are set annually with effect from 1 April, and they go through committee as published papers. Sefton's 2026/27 sports fees appeared as a committee document showing the current charge, the proposed charge and the percentage increase. Oxford publishes the same thing, and Halton's schedule is captioned "subject to approval".</p>
      <p>So next year's council price is a public document months before your renewal letter arrives. Search your council's website for its fees and charges paper and find the sports pages. Universities, trusts and schools do not work this way, so this particular trick is a council one.</p>
      <p>One warning that this guide learned the hard way. A committee paper is a proposal, and the column headings matter: these documents typically show the current year, an inflation-adjusted figure and the proposed new charge side by side. Read the wrong column and you will quote a price that is a year out or one that was never adopted.</p>

      <h2 id="cua">Is the venue bound by a community use agreement?</h2>
      <p>A school or academy sports hall may be covered by a community use agreement that governs its pricing policy, and it is worth finding out because these agreements are public documents.</p>
      <p>Sport England publishes guidance on community use agreements and says they should typically cover hours of availability, management arrangements and pricing policy. It also recommends that such an agreement is secured through a condition on the planning permission for the development, which is the useful part for you: a planning condition is on the public planning record.</p>
      <p>So if you hire a school hall built or extended in the last twenty years or so, look up the school's planning application on your local authority's planning portal and see whether a community use agreement was required. What it says about pricing is specific to that agreement, and we are not going to characterise it for you, because the terms are negotiated per site and Sport England's own material is a template to be adapted rather than a fixed obligation.</p>
      <p>We cannot tell you whether your venue has one. But no other guide on this subject will tell you the lookup exists, and a club that finds one is having a different conversation from a club that opens with "could you do it a bit cheaper".</p>

      <h2 id="terms">Which contract terms matter as much as the rate?</h2>
      <p>Cancellation, variation and notice terms matter as much as the rate does, and this is the one question in this guide where the honest answer is that nobody publishes the standard.</p>
      <p>We went looking for what a normal leisure-centre block-booking agreement contains and found nothing public at any operator. Rather than invent a norm, here is what to get in writing before you commit to a series:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>What notice must <em>they</em> give to cancel or move a session, and what happens to sessions already paid for.</li>
        <li>What notice must <em>you</em> give, and how many sessions you may cancel without losing the block rate. Remember the VAT trade: a series that qualifies for exemption is one you have agreed to pay for whether or not you use it.</li>
        <li>Whether the rate is fixed for the term or reviewed on 1 April regardless.</li>
        <li>What happens when the hall is taken for an exam, a polling station or a school production.</li>
        <li>Whether your allocation carries over automatically at renewal, or is re-offered.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>That last one is worth more attention than it usually gets. A block that has to be reapplied for each year is a block you can lose to whoever asks first.</p>

      <h2 id="ready">What do you need ready before you ask?</h2>
      <p>Have evidence of public liability cover ready, a named person who can sign, and answers to the safeguarding questions a venue will ask if juniors are involved.</p>
      <p>A centre letting a regular block will normally want to see that the club is insured, and for many clubs that cover comes through national or county affiliation rather than a separate policy. We would flag one thing that catches clubs out: club affiliation and individual player membership are different things and cover different people. Do not assume that affiliating the club covers every individual who turns up on a Tuesday, and check what your own affiliation includes, in its current wording, before you tell a centre you are covered.</p>
      <p>Beyond insurance, expect to be asked for some of: a constitution, a bank account in the club's name, a named signatory, your safeguarding policy and DBS position if juniors attend, a risk assessment, first-aid provision and who your first-aider is, and how incidents get reported. Schools ask more of this than leisure centres do, and they ask it earlier.</p>

      <h2 id="walk-away">When should you walk away?</h2>
      <p>Walk away only when there is somewhere else to go, and for a lot of clubs there is not.</p>
      <p>Start with the honest version. Most clubs have one hall that is the right size, in the right place, at the right time of the evening. If that is you, "walk away" is not a lever, and pretending otherwise wastes your time. What you have instead is off-peak, the block structure, and the offer-something-back play above, none of which needs an alternative.</p>
      <p>But if you have not looked, look, because the three cheapest venues in our survey were education venues. School lettings are published by the hour through letting platforms such as SchoolHire, and facility finders including Playfinder and Sport England's Active Places Power will show you what physically exists near you. Both are free and neither requires you to talk to anyone.</p>
      <p><strong>To compare a whole-hall price against a per-court rate you need the court count.</strong> Sport England's design guidance gives a four-court hall as 34.5 by 20 metres, revised up from a previous 33 by 18 metre standard. A listing that gives dimensions can be converted with that. A listing that gives neither dimensions nor court numbers cannot, and we would not guess.</p>
      <p>What you usually give up moving to a school is parking, changing rooms, a bar, and the caretaker's goodwill on the night somebody is ill. Price the move, then decide.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>If you only do one thing</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Work out what your centre charges per <em>hour</em> before you compare it to anything. A 45-minute court at £11.60 is the fourth-cheapest advertised price in our survey and the fourth most expensive in reality.</p>
      </div>

